# Line endings are pinned to LF, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# WHY eol=lf AND NOT THE WINDOWS DEFAULT. Git on Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true: store LF, check out CRLF. That would rewrite all 234
# source files the next time Git touched them, which has two costs here.
# `build.ps1` concatenates src/js/*.js into one file, so every line ending is a
# byte in the deployable artifact — the 5 294 494-character build recorded in
# MASTER-PLAN Part 0 would silently become a different number on a fresh clone.
# And every file would show as modified immediately after checkout.
#
# The whole tree is LF today and the harness has been verified against it, so
# LF is what gets pinned. PowerShell 5.1 reads LF-only .ps1 files perfectly
# well; what it does NOT tolerate is a missing UTF-8 BOM on a script carrying
# Unicode in its patterns (see MASTER-PLAN 1.6) — and `text` handling never
# touches the BOM, so that convention is unaffected.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these, never try to diff them as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
